        
                        PROVOX Version 7.03 Documentation
                             I. General Information
        
        PROVOX7 Program Files. The files for the PROVOX7 program are 
        PROVOX7.EXE, PV7.EXE, and this document, PROVOX7.DOC. There are 
        no command line arguments for PROVOX7.EXE; all command line 
        arguments apply to the PV7.EXE program alone.
        
        Installation. To perform a manual installation, copy the files 
        PROVOX7.EXE and PV7.EXE to your hard disk in a directory on the 
        DOS path. You will want to be able to run PV7 from anywhere; 
        PROVOX7 will be run only once, typically from within CONFIG.SYS 
        or AUTOEXEC.BAT, and therefore it need not be on the command 
        path. Place a line in the CONFIG.SYS file that looks like this:
                         DEVICE=C:\WHEREVER\PROVOX7.EXE
        substituting the name of the appropriate drive and directory for 
        C:\WHEREVER. Next reboot the system and listen for the sound 
        signature, three rising tones, as the PROVOX7 device driver 
        successfully installs. Although installed, PROVOX7 will remain 
        dormant until activated by the PV7 program. You may run PV7 from 
        the DOS prompt, or place it in the AUTOEXEC.BAT file for 
        automatic execution on system startup.
        
        The PV7 program accepts optional command line arguments to 
        control PROVOX7, but one argument you must use is the name of the 
        speech device. Commands of the following form are presently 
        supported:
                                   PV7 DECTALK
                                    PV7 DTPC
                                PV7 AUDAPTER LPT1
                                PV7 LITETALK COM2
                                    PV7 DTLT
        
        Every speech device is associated with a default output port. If 
        the default output port is the one you wish to use, you may omit 
        it from the PV7 command line. Caution: Not all devices support 
        all ports, so read on. The following list gives the supported 
        synthesizer names and their default output ports:
        
                                  ACCENT <LPT3>
                                 AUDAPTER <COM1>
                                   BNS <COM1>
                                 BRLMATE <COM1>
                            DECTALK <simulated COM4>
                               DTLT <actual COM#.>
                                   DTPC (none)
                                 LITETALK <LPT1>
                                 PORTTALK <LPT3>
                                   PSS <COM1>
        
        For the DECTALK device, only the internal board is supported. For 
        the DTLT device, the correct COM port will automatically be 
        located if the device is turned on when PROVOX7 installs. For 
        internal boards with drivers that simulate output ports such as 

        the Accent PC and the Artic SynPhonix boards using Porttalk 
        software, it is recommended that a simulated COM port be used 
        instead of a simulated LPT port. If this is not possible, PROVOX7 
        should not be installed as a device driver, but as a normal TSR 
        program in the AUTOEXEC.BAT file instead. The command line to run 
        PROVOX7 as a TSR should follow the command line to run the port 
        emulation software. In that case both simulated LPT ports and 
        simulated COM ports will work properly. The available output port 
        choices are: LPT1, LPT2, LPT3, LPT4, COM1, COM2, COM3, COM4.
        
        When PROVOX7 is run it copies the existing contents of the screen 
        to a buffer, including any text that may have preceded its own 
        banner. It also saves any subsequent screen output while waiting 
        to be activated by the PV7 program. When PV7 is run it checks to 
        see whether PROVOX7 is a device driver or a memory resident TSR. 
        If it is a device driver, PV7 writes the saved material to a file 
        called PV7.LOG in the directory where PV7.EXE is located. You can 
        examine this file to determine what was displayed on the screen 
        before speech was activated. If PROVOX7 is a TSR program, PV7 
        reads the saved information, but does not save it in a file. This 
        makes it possible to run PROVOX7 and PV7 safely from a write-
        protected diskette if necessary.
        
        At the present time the available PV7 commands in addition to the 
        speech device name and output port are:
                                    PV7 HELP
                                    PV7 INIT
                                    PV7 SAVE
                                   PV7 RESTORE
                                    PV7 QUIET
                                    PV7 SPEAK
                                   PV7 STATUS
        Other PV7 commands may be added in future releases.
        
        Editing System Files. When adding the DEVICE= line to CONFIG.SYS,  
        be sure to place it after the line that installs a memory manager 
        such as QEMM or EMM386. This assures that the PROVOX7 device 
        driver will be loaded high if possible by DOS MEMMAKER or a 
        similar utility. When adding the PV7 startup command to 
        AUTOEXEC.BAT, you should place it toward the end of the file so 
        as to suppress the speaking of possibly lengthy startup messages 
        from other system components. These messages will be written to 
        the PV7.LOG file for later reference if you need them. If the PV7 
        command precedes the PATH command within the AUTOEXEC.BAT file, 
        you will have to include its location in the command like this:
                            C:\WHEREVER\PV7 <device>
        substituting the drive and directory name for c:\WHEREVER. Do not 
        omit the extension .EXE from the DEVICE= command that references 
        the PROVOX7 program. You may optionally include or omit such 
        extensions from program names that are referenced in AUTOEXEC.BAT 
        but they are required in CONFIG.SYS.
        
        The PV7 SAVE command can be used to store all the current 
        settings of PROVOX7 for later recall with the PV7 RESTORE 
        command. If the name of the speech synthesizer and the output 

        port have been saved in this way, the AUTOEXEC.BAT file may 
        contain a command like:
                                   PV7 RESTORE
        
        The PV7 INIT command may be given at any time to re-initialize 
        the speech device. The INIT function will occur automatically 
        when exiting from Windows, or when using RESTORE or a device name 
        or port name with the PV7 program.
        
                       II. PROVOX7 Commands and Features.
        
        Prefix Key. The Slash is the default Prefix key. You may change 
        to the Accent key or to the Escape key by pressing Prefix Accent 
        or Prefix Escape respectively. You may change back again by 
        pressing Prefix Slash. To enter a real Slash, a real Accent, or a 
        real Escape  when it is being used as the Prefix key, simply 
        press the key twice in succession.
        
        Modes of Operation. PROVOX7 detects the presence of an enhanced 
        keyboard and automatically selects Enhanced Mode if one is found. 
        In enhanced mode the numeric keypad is used for PROVOX7 commands, 
        and only the keys between the numeric keypad and the standard 
        QWERTY keys control the PC cursor. The older Dual Mode (Direct 
        mode and Review mode) is available by entering Prefix NumLock. To 
        determine whether PROVOX7 is in Enhanced Mode or Dual Mode, 
        simply press NumLock. If it says only "NUMLOCK" it is in Enhanced 
        Mode. If it alternately says "NUMLOCK ON" and "NUMLOCK OFF" it is 
        in Dual Mode. You cannot change the PC NumLock State in Enhanced 
        Mode, although you may press a shift key to enter numbers there.
        
        Many notebook computers report having an enhanced keyboard even 
        though they do not have a dual set of cursor keys. To use PROVOX7 
        conveniently on such computers, while PROVOX7 is running turn off 
        enhanced mode with Prefix NumLock, and use the PV7 SAVE command 
        to record this change. You may then activate PROVOX7 from within 
        the AUTOEXEC.BAT file with the PV7 RESTORE command and the normal 
        Dual Mode operation will be available.
        
             Some of the following commands use a letter such as L or T. 
        Either upper or lower case may be used to type them. Some 
        commands use function keys. These are shown below as F1, F2, 
        Shift F1, Shift F2, and so on. Still other commands use a 
        punctuation mark or one or two digits. The name of the 
        punctuation mark (e.g. Period or Equals) or the word Digit (or 
        Digit Digit) are shown in the commands below. Finally, a few 
        commands use a Control key in combination with another key. The 
        word Control is used below to indicate that. When using Shift or 
        Control, you may use either the left or the right key, and you 
        must press it first, hold it down, then press the other key that 
        goes with it, releasing them in either order. 
        
        Prefix Period -- Gives the cursor location. Column One Line One 
        is the top left corner of the screen.
        
        Prefix L -- Reads the current line.

        
        Prefix T -- Gives the day of the week, the month, the day, and 
        the time of day.
        
        Prefix C -- Names the current character.
        
        Prefix W -- Reads the current word.
        
        Prefix S -- Reads the entire screen.
        
        Prefix H -- Reads only the highlighted information in the 
        selected subscreen. Follow the H with a letter, a digit, or a 
        punctuation mark indicating the subscreen from which to read 
        highlights; e.g. Prefix HS, Prefix HL, Prefix H+, Prefix H2, etc.
        
        Prefix G -- Grabs text from the screen and puts it onto the 
        clipboard. The text from the review mode cursor to the end of the 
        line is grabbed. Control characters and upper ASCII characters 
        are replaced by spaces, and any leading or consecutive spaces are 
        suppressed. Successive grabs put additional material onto the 
        clipboard, up to a maximum of 512 characters. The count of 
        characters in the clipboard is reported.
        
        Prefix K -- Adds a carriage return to the end of the clipboard. 
        If the last character in the clipboard is already a carriage 
        return, the clipboard is emptied. The count of characters in the 
        clipboard is reported.
        
        Prefix P -- Pastes the clipboard data one character at a time 
        onto the current screen by "stuffing the keyboard buffer." 
        Pasting copies the data but does not remove it from the 
        clipboard. This command is only valid in Direct Mode.
        
        Prefix R -- Reports yhow many seconds have elapsed since Provox7 
        was run, or since the last Prefix Z command was used. 
        
        Prefix Z -- Resets the Prefix R count to zero.
        
        Prefix F Digit -- Reads only the information on the screen in a 
        given Foreground attribute. The digit indicates the frequency of 
        the foreground; 1 means the most common, 2 means the second most 
        common, 3 means the third most common, etc.
        
        Prefix FF Digit -- Names the given Foreground attribute. The 
        digit indicates the frequency of the foreground; 1 names the most 
        common, 2 names the second most common, 3 names the third most 
        common, etc.
        
        Prefix B Digit --  Reads only the information on the screen in a 
        given Background attribute. The digit indicates the frequency of 
        the Background; 1 means the most common, 2 means the second most 
        common, 3 means the third most common, etc.
        Prefix BB Digit --  Names the given Background attribute. The 
        digit indicates the frequency of the Background; 1 names the most 
        common, 2 names the second most common, 3 names the third most 

        common, etc.
        
        Prefix Tab Digit -- Reads the information enclosed within the 
        borders of a box on the screen. The digit indicates which of 
        several possible boxes to read; 1 means the first box found, 2 
        means the second box found, 3 means the third box found, etc.
        
        Prefix Tab Tab Digit --  Gives the location of the corners of a 
        box on the screen. The digit indicates which of several possible 
        boxes to locate; 1 locates the first box found, 2 locates the 
        second box found, 3 locates the third box found, etc.
        
        Prefix N Digit -- Gives the name of the specified Screen Bank. 
        Press Enter instead of a digit to get the name of the currently 
        active Screen Bank.
        
        Prefix Question Digit -- Describes the location and filter 
        settings of a specified subscreen within the currently active 
        Screen Bank.
        
        Prefix Equals Digit -- Lets you define a specified subscreen 
        within the currently active screenbank. This command uses corners 
        which must be marked previously with Prefix Shift F3 and Prefix 
        Shift F4.
        
        Prefix PgDn -- Begins to issue Down Arrow keystrokes at the rate 
        of about one per second, for continuously reading a document 
        while in a word processor, editor, or browser. The next key 
        pressed, including other PROVOX commands, terminates the process.
        
        Prefix PrtSc -- Writes an ASCII file named PV######.TXT in the 
        root directory of drive C: with the contents of the screen. The 
        six # characters in the filename are replaced by two digits for 
        the year, two for the month, and two for the day. The file is 
        created if it does not already exist, and is appended to if it 
        already exists.
        
        Prefix Shift PrtSc -- Writes a binary file named PV######.BIN in 
        the root directory of drive C: with the exact contents of the 
        screen. The six # characters in the filename are replaced by two 
        digits for the year, two for the month, and two for the day. The 
        file is created if it does not already exist, and is appended to 
        if it already exists. While this file cannot be accessed with an 
        ASCII editor or word processor, it can be examined with the DOS 
        DEBUG command or a Hex Editor to inspect the character and 
        attribute values in video memory.
        
        Prefix Scroll Lock -- Disables PROVOX and restores normal 
        keyboard operation. PROVOX may be reactivated from a DOS prompt 
        with the PV7 SPEAK command.
        
        Prefix E -- Activates the E-Mail Filter. When activated, most 
        electronic mail address lines are written silently to the screen. 
        For the most part, only the content of E-mail messages is 
        directly spoken. Repeating the command closes the filter.

        
        Prefix O -- Activates OpenPort. The user is asked for the number 
        of a COM Port to Open and may choose 1, 2, 3, or 4. If the 
        selected port exists and is not in use by PROVOX7, it is opened 
        and connected to the keyboard buffer. This feature permits 
        uploading text files from such devices as Braille 'N Speak or the 
        Kurzweil Reading Edge directly into a word processor. Start the 
        word processor, press Prefix O, select the port number, and send 
        the file. Press Prefix O again when finished to resume normal 
        operation. You may then edit the file and save it as a document 
        on your PC. When OpenPort is active and no data are being 
        received, a reminder beep is heard every 14 seconds. You should 
        try not to use the keyboard while data are being received, except 
        to kill pending speech with ALT or CONTROL. These are the 
        technical specifications: The port is set to 9600 BPS, eight data 
        bits, no parity, one stop bit. Ports 1 and 3 use IRQ 4, while 
        ports 2 and 4 use IRQ3. Hardware handshaking is implemented with 
        RTS and CTS. 
        
        Prefix INS AnyKey -- Reports the ASCII value and the Scan Code of 
        any key pressed after the Insert key.
        
        Prefix END -- Reads the lowest non-blank line of the screen 
        without moving the PC cursor.
        
        Prefix Home -- Reads the highest non-blank line of the screen 
        without moving the PC cursor.
        
        Prefix Left or Right Arrow -- Reads the character adjoining the 
        current cursor position in the corresponding direction without 
        changing the cursor location.
        
        Prefix Up or Down Arrow -- Reads the nearest non-blank line 
        adjoining the current cursor position in the corresponding 
        direction.
        
        Prefix Control Left or Control Right Arrow -- Reads the nearest 
        word to the left or to the right of the current cursor position 
        without changing its location.
        
        Prefix Star -- Names the current character, gives its location on 
        the screen, and describes its foreground and background 
        attributes.
        
        Prefix Digit -- Reads the contents of the specified subscreen 
        within the active Screen Bank.
        
        Prefix Plus -- Reads the screen from the current line through the 
        bottom line.
        Prefix Dash -- Reads the screen from the top line through the 
        current line.
        
        Prefix Less Than -- Reads the current line from the left end to 
        the cursor position.
        

        Prefix Greater Than -- Reads the current line from the cursor 
        position to the right end.
        
        Prefix F1 -- Reviews the meanings and current settings of the 
        PROVOX7 Function Key commands.
        
        Prefix F2 -- Reports the current settings of keyboard lock keys 
        and several other system features.
        
        Prefix F3 -- Controls the speaking of numbers; 0 means read whole 
        numbers (123 = one hundred twenty three), 1 means read numbers 
        digit by digit (123 = one two three).
        
        Prefix F4 -- Controls the speaking of capitalization; 0 means 
        ignore capitalization, 1 means say the word CAP before every 
        capital letter.
        
        Prefix F5 -- Controls the speaking of punctuation; 0 passes all 
        data unmodified to the speech device; 1 pronounces only letters 
        and digits; 2 pronounces letters, digits, punctuation marks, and 
        spaces typed, but not spaces read from the screen; 3 pronounces 
        everything.
        
        Prefix F6 Digit -- Reports and lets you set the Smart Screen 
        level; 0 means no Smart Screen features, 1 means Auto Ignore one 
        or two specific subscreens, 2 means Auto Alert a specific 
        subscreen, 3 means read all minor background data, 4 means read 
        only the least common background data, 5 means read all minor 
        foreground data, and 6 means read only the least common 
        foreground data. Settings larger than one also automatically read 
        Pop Up boxes when they first appear, when their contents change,  
        or when they change position on the screen.
        
        Prefix F7 -- Sets the Keyboard Echo level; 0 means do not echo 
        keystrokes, 1 means echo each keystroke and kill all waiting 
        speech, 2 means wait for words to form before echoing keystrokes 
        (useful in online chat mode).
        
        Prefix F8 -- Sets the Screen Echo level; 0 means do not echo 
        screen data, 1 means echo screen data when detected, 2 means wait 
        for words to form before echoing screen data (useful in online 
        chat mode).
        
        Prefix F9 -- Sets speech synthesizer speed.
        
        Prefix F10 -- Sets speech synthesizer volume.
        
        Prefix Shift F1 -- Reviews the meanings and current settings of 
        the PROVOX Shift Function Key commands.
        
        Prefix Shift F2 -- Offers a menu of options to change speech 
        parameters and subscreen assignments for Auto Alert and Auto 
        Ignore.
        
        Prefix Shift F3 -- Marks one diagonal corner of a screen area 

        using the review cursor location, for later use with the Prefix 
        Equals command.
        
        Prefix Shift F4 -- Marks the other diagonal corner of a screen 
        area using the review cursor location, for later use with the 
        Prefix Equals command.
        
        Prefix Shift F5 Digit -- Selects a Screen Bank to be loaded; a 
        digit from 0 to 9 identifies the Screen Bank.
        
        Prefix Shift F6 Digit -- Selects a Screen Bank to be saved; a 
        digit from 0 to 9 identifies the Screen Bank.
        
        Prefix Shift F9 Digit Digit -- Reports and/or resets the screen 
        column where an audible margin beep is produced on keyboard 
        input.
        
        Prefix Shift F10 Digit -- Reports and/or resets the WatchDog 
        Threshold.
        
        The following commands use keys on the Numeric Keypad. They do 
        not use the usual PROVOX7 Prefix key. If you are using an 
        application that requires a Keypad key for one of its commands, 
        you may enter it by first pressing the Keypad Slash, then the 
        desired key. This lets you "escape" from the Enhanced Mode for 
        that single entry.
        
        Keypad Period -- Reports the current PROVOX7 Review Cursor 
        location.
        
        Keypad Enter -- Moves the PROVOX7 Review Cursor to the location 
        of the PC System Cursor.
        
        Keypad Control Enter --Moves the PC System Cursor to the location 
        of the PROVOX7 Review Cursor if possible, by issuing a series of 
        Left, Right, Up, or Down Arrow keystrokes. You need to be running 
        an appropriate application to use this "cursor routing" feature, 
        since DOS generally does not recognize arrow keys directly.
        
        Keypad Plus --Reads the contents of the line where the PROVOX7 
        Review Cursor resides.
        
        Keypad Dash -- Reads the word where the PROVOX Review7 Cursor 
        resides.
        
        Keypad Star -- Names the character where the PROVOX7 Review 
        Cursor resides, gives its screen location, and describes its 
        attributes.
        
        Keypad Slash -- Permits the next character typed to escape from 
        PROVOX7 and be sent directly to the application.
        
        Keypad NumLock -- Does nothing in Enhanced Mode; changes NumLock 
        State in Dual (Direct and Review) Mode.
        

        Keypad Left, Right, Up, or Down Arrow -- Moves the PROVOX7 Review 
        Cursor in the corresponding direction.
        
        Keypad Home, End, PgUp, or PgDn -- Moves the PROVOX7 Review 
        Cursor to the corresponding edges or corners of the screen.
        
        Keypad Center Key -- Names the character at the location of the 
        PROVOX7 Review Cursor; every other press of this key on the same 
        character gives its phonetic pronunciation.
        
        Keypad INS -- Permits entering a Scan Target to search for on the 
        screen.
        
        Keypad Digit Digit -- Moves to the left end of the specified 
        screen line and reads its contents. To enter a digit on the 
        Keypad in Enhanced Mode, press a Shift key together with the 
        desired number.
        
        Keypad Control Left or Control Right Arrow -- Moves the PROVOX7 
        Review Cursor to the previous word or to the next word on the 
        screen and reads it.
        
        Keypad Control Home or Control End -- Searches the screen 
        backward or forward for the specified Scan Target.
        
        Keypad Control PgUp or Control PgDn -- Searches the screen 
        backward or forward for the next encountered change of screen 
        attribute and reports it.
        
                         III. Automatic PROVOX Features
        
        The PROVOX7 program has several features that operate without 
        explicit commands to invoke them. Some of them are described in 
        the following paragraphs.
        
        Screen Size Settings. If the video display adapter on your PC is 
        EGA or VGA, the screen may be set to display 43 or 50 lines of 
        text instead of the usual 25. PROVOX7 automatically recognizes 
        this setting and adapts to the larger screen sizes. While some 
        screen readers use a letter from "A" to "Y" to refer to the 25 
        lines of the standard screen, PROVOX7 uses a two digit number and 
        therefore adapts easily to larger displays. The screen capture 
        commands and those that refer to the bottom line of the screen 
        also adapt automatically to these settings.
        
        ScreenBank Switching. Every time you press the ENTER key, PROVOX7 
        examines the current screen line to the left of the cursor to 
        determine if you have just entered a DOS command that matches one 
        of its ten ScreenBank names. If a match is found, the ScreenBank 
        is activated and the word SCREENBANK is spoken. If several 
        seconds pass without pressing any keys this matching process is 
        also attempted, so that when returning to a DOS prompt PROVOX7 
        can restore its original ScreenBank settings. For this feature to 
        work properly, three things are required:
        

        (1) you must be using the default DOS prompt, usually achieved by 
        placing "PROMPT $P$G" in the AUTOEXEC.BAT file. 
        
        (2) the ScreenBank names must include an initial GreaterThan 
        character, such as >WP, >FIRST, or >PROCOMM.
        
        (3) when typing a DOS command there must be no space between the 
        GreaterThan of the DOS prompt and the first letter of the command 
        name.
        
        Microsoft Windows Watch. If PROVOX7 is installed on a PC which 
        runs Windows under DOS, it will detect and report the onset and 
        the termination of Windows execution, and return to normal 
        performance after Windows terminates. The Windows version is also 
        reported.
        
        Enhanced Text Parsing. While PROVOX7 leaves most of the text to 
        speech translation to the synthesizer device being used, two 
        additional steps in the process are performed by PROVOX7 
        automatically. First, radio amateur call signs are always 
        spelled. An amateur call sign is recognized if it has a one or 
        two letter prefix, a single digit in the middle, and a one to 
        three letter suffix. Second, mixed case words in which a capital 
        letter follows a lower case letter are divided into parts at that 
        point. Thus the words dBASE, GetS, and cRun will all be spoken as 
        two syllable words.
        
        Caps Lock Monitor. When the keyboard Caps Lock is off and you 
        press a shifted letter key the PC recognizes an upper case 
        letter. When Caps Lock is on and you do that, it recognizes a 
        lower case letter instead of an upper case one. Since it is 
        extremely easy to forget that Caps Lock is on, or to turn it on 
        accidentally, PROVOX7 issues a warning beep if you enter a 
        shifted letter while the Caps Lock is on. Maybe that was what you 
        wanted, or maybe your life or reputation has just been saved.
        
        Repetition Counter. When either a single character or a pair of 
        characters is repeated more than three times in succession, 
        PROVOX7 says only the first three instances. It then waits until 
        the sequence is broken, then gives the repeat count.
        
        Buffer Overflow. PROVOX7 maintains a large buffer in which to 
        accumulate text to be spoken. If this buffer becomes full, 
        PROVOX7 gives an audible warning tone and then empties the 
        buffer. The information waiting to be spoken is lost, but you are 
        alerted and the PC is not delayed.
        
        Blank Lines. PROVOX7 says the word BLANK when it encounters a 
        blank line while reading the screen.
        
